  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Missing object-level authorization in ARIA `query_account`",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing object-level authorization in ARIA `query_account`"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The ARIA `query_account(user_id)` tool accepts a caller-supplied user ID and returns the matching account profile and transaction history without verifying that the requested record belongs to the logged-in user. Any authenticated customer can use the tool to read other customers' or staff records."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The ARIA `query_account(user_id)` tool accepts a caller-supplied user ID and returns the matching account profile and transaction history without verifying that the requested record belongs to the logged-in user. Any authenticated customer can use the tool to read other customers' or staff records.\n\nA logged-in attacker can disclose another person's balance, profile details, fraud flags, and transaction history, including staff accounts. That breaks account isolation and exposes sensitive banking data across user and role boundaries.\n\nBind account lookups to the server-side authenticated identity before any database read. Pass the current session user ID into the tool builder, reject any mismatched `user_id`, and add a regression test that confirms cross-user and staff IDs are denied even when the caller knows the identifier.",
                "markdown": "The ARIA `query_account(user_id)` tool accepts a caller-supplied user ID and returns the matching account profile and transaction history without verifying that the requested record belongs to the logged-in user. Any authenticated customer can use the tool to read other customers' or staff records.\n\nA logged-in attacker can disclose another person's balance, profile details, fraud flags, and transaction history, including staff accounts. That breaks account isolation and exposes sensitive banking data across user and role boundaries.\n\nBind account lookups to the server-side authenticated identity before any database read. Pass the current session user ID into the tool builder, reject any mismatched `user_id`, and add a regression test that confirms cross-user and staff IDs are denied even when the caller knows the identifier."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Missing authorization in ARIA tools exposes other customers' accounts and internal policy data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing authorization in ARIA tools exposes other customers' accounts and internal policy data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A low-privilege chat user can steer ARIA into calling `query_account` and `lookup_policy` with attacker-chosen arguments. Because the tools do not enforce ownership or role checks, the assistant discloses another customer's full account and the restricted internal treasury document."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A low-privilege chat user can steer ARIA into calling `query_account` and `lookup_policy` with attacker-chosen arguments. Because the tools do not enforce ownership or role checks, the assistant discloses another customer's full account and the restricted internal treasury document.\n\nAn attacker can read another customer's balance, contact details, fraud flags, and transaction history, then pivot to internal treasury, reserve-ratio, and fraud-override data that should be limited to Finance, Risk, and Compliance. The disclosure materially increases the risk of targeted fraud, phishing, and operational intelligence gathering.\n\nEnforce caller-bound checks in `query_account` so the tool can only read the signed-in customer's own record, and restrict `lookup_policy` to customer-facing topics unless the caller is explicitly staff. Keep the authorization check in the tool layer itself; model instructions are not a security boundary. Align the system prompt with the real policy so it does not imply that the tools handle authorization for the application.",
                "markdown": "A low-privilege chat user can steer ARIA into calling `query_account` and `lookup_policy` with attacker-chosen arguments. Because the tools do not enforce ownership or role checks, the assistant discloses another customer's full account and the restricted internal treasury document.\n\nAn attacker can read another customer's balance, contact details, fraud flags, and transaction history, then pivot to internal treasury, reserve-ratio, and fraud-override data that should be limited to Finance, Risk, and Compliance. The disclosure materially increases the risk of targeted fraud, phishing, and operational intelligence gathering.\n\nEnforce caller-bound checks in `query_account` so the tool can only read the signed-in customer's own record, and restrict `lookup_policy` to customer-facing topics unless the caller is explicitly staff. Keep the authorization check in the tool layer itself; model instructions are not a security boundary. Align the system prompt with the real policy so it does not imply that the tools handle authorization for the application."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "Name-only login allows account impersonation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Name-only login allows account impersonation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The login form authenticates NeoBank users by matching only the entered full name, so any known customer or staff name becomes a valid credential. An attacker who knows or guesses a real name can sign in as that person and inherit their session identity."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The login form authenticates NeoBank users by matching only the entered full name, so any known customer or staff name becomes a valid credential. An attacker who knows or guesses a real name can sign in as that person and inherit their session identity.\n\nA low-privileged attacker can impersonate another customer or a staff account with no password, token, or second factor. That lets the attacker act under the victim's identity in the workshop app and opens the door to any identity-bound actions the UI or tools expose to that session.\n\nRequire a second factor that is not publicly guessable, such as a user ID plus name, a one-time code, or another verified secret. Update the login form to collect the additional factor and switch the authentication call to a verifier that checks both fields before creating the session. Do not treat a display name as sufficient proof of identity.",
                "markdown": "The login form authenticates NeoBank users by matching only the entered full name, so any known customer or staff name becomes a valid credential. An attacker who knows or guesses a real name can sign in as that person and inherit their session identity.\n\nA low-privileged attacker can impersonate another customer or a staff account with no password, token, or second factor. That lets the attacker act under the victim's identity in the workshop app and opens the door to any identity-bound actions the UI or tools expose to that session.\n\nRequire a second factor that is not publicly guessable, such as a user ID plus name, a one-time code, or another verified secret. Update the login form to collect the additional factor and switch the authentication call to a verifier that checks both fields before creating the session. Do not treat a display name as sufficient proof of identity."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            },
